WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.710
For the last century, medical science has largely treated the human body like a machine.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:12.670
If a part isn't working, we invent a synthetic chemical in a lab and use it to force a

3
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.010
receptor into submission.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.710
But this mechanical approach carries a massive human cost.

5
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.530
When you treat complex biological systems as simple chemical equations,

6
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.670
you get catastrophic failures.

7
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.090
The opioid epidemic is a devastating example of what happens when we try to bludgeon the

8
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.870
body's receptors into compliance.

9
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:37.470
There is a hidden flaw in how we make modern biologic drugs too.

10
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.930
Traditional manufacturing relies on heat to sterilize and process materials.

11
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.490
But biological proteins are delicate three-dimensional structures.

12
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.270
When you expose them to heat,

13
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:49.430
they denature.

14
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.790
They literally unfold and fall apart.

15
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.570
In biology, form is function.

16
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.690
If you destroy a protein's physical shape during processing, you destroy its ability to

17
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.670
deliver the right message to the patient.

18
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.210
Boris Markosian looks at the body entirely differently.

19
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:11.030
He argues the human body isn't a collection of independent mechanical parts to be blocked

20
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:11.830
or forced.

21
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.130
It is a highly interconnected language of signals.

22
00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.550
This perspective is the foundation of a new therapeutic field called Instructional Medicine.

23
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.050
Instead of introducing foreign chemicals, the goal is to heal the body by using the natural

24
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.210
signaling proteins it already produces,

25
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.810
isolating them with their physical structures completely intact.

26
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.570
But translating that biological language isn't simple.

27
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.290
To crack it, you need someone who understands the physical realities of handling delicate

28
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.710
organic tissue, and who can also build the computational architecture required to map

29
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.950
millions of molecular interactions.

30
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.210
Markosian represents that rare cross-disciplinary overlap.

31
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.850
His career spans both intensive laboratory science and massive data operations,

32
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.690
positioning him to translate the instructions the medical establishment has overlooked.

33
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.610
Markosian's understanding of physical biology started with his studies in physiology and

34
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.570
neuroscience at UC San Diego.

35
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.710
From there, he moved into high-stakes wet lab environments, conducting cancer immunotherapy

36
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.230
research at the UCSD Moores Cancer Center, and working on oncolytic virotherapy as a

37
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.530
scientist at Genelux.

38
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.950
He also gained crucial operational experience managing complex laboratory environments

39
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.630
at the Salk Institute for Biological Studies.

40
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:38.090
Spending a decade handling delicate organic materials showed him firsthand exactly why

41
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.850
traditional processing methods fail.

42
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.470
He saw how standard techniques structurally destroy the very signaling molecules researchers

43
00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.670
are trying to study.

44
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.890
Then he did something unusual for a biological researcher.

45
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.990
He stepped out of the laboratory entirely and took a role as a data operations lead at

46
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:56.430
Google.

47
00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:02.170
In this corporate tech environment, he learned how to manage, map, and scale impossibly

48
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.050
complex information architectures.

49
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.490
He was learning how to organize massive data sets,

50
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:12.510
skills directly applicable to the millions of signaling pathways in the human body.

51
00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:18.910
He first attempted to merge biology and data by founding Vaxenta Biotechnologies at Johnson

52
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.610
& Johnson's JLabs, focusing on a cancer vaccine thesis.

53
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.850
Later, he launched Placenta Products LLC,

54
00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.650
bringing an oral supplement to market to test the practical delivery of biological signaling

55
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.450
molecules.

56
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.890
These early ventures proved a crucial concept.

57
00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:40.210
Biological signals carry vital information that standard chemical and nutritional models

58
00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:47.090
completely miss. 15 years of research recently culminated in a massive 2026

59
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.590
master intellectual property portfolio.

60
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.590
Markosian prepared and filed it himself as the sole inventor,

61
00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:55.550
without outside legal counsel.

62
00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:01.710
The portfolio includes six patent applications, built from over 200 drafts, designed to

63
00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.970
lay the groundwork for an entirely new therapeutic ecosystem.

64
00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.950
The first half of this system is computational.

65
00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.970
It's called the Advanced Science AI Platform, or ASAP,

66
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.970
and it's designed to map exactly how the human body reasons on a molecular level.

67
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.370
Take a look at these pathway diagrams.

68
00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.950
This is ASAP's Signaling Cascade Knowledge Graph.

69
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.770
It tracks how specific biological ingredients trigger direct outcomes.

70
00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.670
On the left, glowing connective lines map the pathway from an ingredient through receptors

71
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.450
to an indication, like a diabetic foot ulcer.

72
00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:40.350
On the right, a similar route maps to wound healing.

73
00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.270
Notice the text at the bottom.

74
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.390
Standard databases only list facts, but this engine applies hyperbolic geometry to actively

75
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.090
forecast disease directions,

76
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.150
tracking every prediction back to its source literature.

77
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.050
Because before you can deliver a natural biological instruction to a patient, you must

78
00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:02.470
first have the advanced computational map required to know which instruction to send.

79
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.090
Once the right instruction is identified,

80
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.350
the second half of the system has to physically extract it.

81
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.030
This is the Markosian High-Fidelity Bio-Processing Protocol.

82
00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:17.990
To solve the denaturing problem, this protocol utilizes a proprietary thermodynamic cold

83
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:18.350
stop.

84
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.590
It isolates heat-labile signaling peptides without ever using the destructive heat that

85
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.570
ruins standard biologics. Markosian has engineered the two necessary halves,

86
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.590
the computational brain to find the exact biological signals, and the physical engine

87
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.610
to extract them with their structures perfectly intact.

88
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.510
The long-term objective here is to move away from mass-produced synthetic chemicals that

89
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.250
treat the average patient, who doesn't actually exist.

90
00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:49.450
Instead, the goal is bespoke therapeutics,

91
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.130
dosed and tailored to the unique biology of the individual.

92
00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:58.350
The platform's future involves payload-agnostic engineered exosomes,

93
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.510
which will safely carry these natural biologics directly to where the body needs them.

94
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:04.910
Look at this illustration.

95
00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.790
It perfectly represents this new approach,

96
00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:13.290
matching data-driven computation on the left with natural, complex protein structures

97
00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:14.070
on the right.

98
00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:17.630
It captures the body's messages in their original form,

99
00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.650
resulting in medicine that is endogenous by design.

100
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.170
It all points back to Markosian's core philosophy.

101
00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.030
The body already knows how to heal itself.

102
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.950
We simply provide the reminder.

103
00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:35.250
Sometimes a genuine medical shift requires someone who sits outside the traditional silos,

104
00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:41.310
a cross-disciplinary pioneer willing to finally learn and speak the language the medical

105
00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.630
establishment has forgotten.

